    unitId: 'cp-resp',
    specialty: 'Respiratory Disorders',
    overview: 'Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) is an acute infection of the lung parenchyma acquired outside of hospital or within 48 hours of admission, most commonly caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae (the leading bacterial pathogen), Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, and atypical organisms (Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, Legionella pneumophila). Viral causes (influenza, SARS-CoV-2, RSV) are increasingly recognised, particularly in the post-pandemic era. Severity assessment using CURB-65 (Confusion, Urea >7 mmol/L, RR ≥30, BP <90/60, age ≥65) guides site of care: score 0-1 = outpatient, 2 = consider hospitalisation, 3-5 = hospitalise/ICU. Empiric antibiotic therapy: outpatient (amoxicillin 500 mg-1 g three times daily for 5-7 days, or doxycycline 100 mg twice daily, or macrolide if local resistance <25%); non-ICU inpatient (amoxicillin/clavulanate 625 mg three times daily or ceftriaxone 1-2 g daily + azithromycin 500 mg daily, or levofloxacin 750 mg daily monotherapy); ICU (beta-lactam + macrolide or fluoroquinolone). The WHO AWaRe classification places amoxicillin in the Access group and ceftriaxone in the Watch group, emphasising stewardship.',
    diagram: CAP_DIAGRAM,
    keyDrugs: [
      { drug: 'Amoxicillin 500 mg-1 g three times daily (outpatient)', class: 'Aminopenicillin (AWaRe: Access)', sideEffects: ['Rash (non-allergic maculopapular)', 'Diarrhoea', 'Anaphylaxis (rare, 1-5 per 100,000)', 'Nausea', 'C. difficile colitis (uncommon)'] },
      { drug: 'Ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily (inpatient)', class: 'Third-generation cephalosporin (AWaRe: Watch)', sideEffects: ['Biliary sludge/pseudolithiasis', 'Diarrhoea', 'C. difficile infection', 'Allergic cross-reactivity with penicillin (1-2%)', 'Coombs-positive haemolytic anaemia', 'Neutropenia (prolonged use)'] },
      { drug: 'Azithromycin 500 mg daily (3-5 days)', class: 'Macrolide', sideEffects: ['QT prolongation (risk with other QT-prolonging drugs)', 'GI upset (nausea, diarrhoea, abdominal pain)', 'Hepatotoxicity (cholestatic)', 'Ototoxicity (high doses)', 'Clostridioides difficile'] },
      { drug: 'Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily (5-7 days)', class: 'Tetracycline', sideEffects: ['Photosensitivity (severe sunburn)', 'Oesophageal ulceration (take upright with water)', 'Tooth discolouration (contraindicated <8 years)', 'Hepatotoxicity', 'Vestibular toxicity (dizziness)'] },
      { drug: 'Levofloxacin 750 mg daily (5-7 days)', class: 'Respiratory fluoroquinolone', sideEffects: ['QT prolongation', 'Tendon rupture (risk increased with steroids/age >60)', 'C. difficile infection', 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Aortic aneurysm/dissection (rare)', 'Phototoxicity'] },
    ],
    monitoring: 'Temperature chart until afebrile (typically 48-72 hours). White blood cell count and C-reactive protein at baseline and 48-72 hours (CRP >100 mg/L at day 3 suggests treatment failure). Blood cultures (×2 sets) before antibiotics in hospitalised patients. Sputum culture and Gram stain if productive cough. CURB-65 reassessment at 24-48 hours. Clinical response: improvement in cough, dyspnoea, oxygenation within 48-72 hours. Follow-up chest X-ray at 6 weeks to confirm radiological resolution (especially to exclude underlying malignancy in smokers >50 years). Pulse oximetry and SpO2 monitoring. Renal function if on aminoglycosides or IV contrast. Assess for parapneumonic effusion if slow improvement.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'What is the recommended empiric antibiotic regimen for a hospitalised (non-ICU) CAP patient with no comorbidities?', options: ['A. Amoxicillin monotherapy', 'B. Ceftriaxone IV + Azithromycin IV/oral', 'C. Metronidazole + Gentamicin', 'D. Co-amoxiclav + Metronidazole'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'For non-ICU inpatient CAP, a beta-lactam (ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily) plus a macrolide (azithromycin 500 mg daily) provides coverage for S. pneumoniae and atypical pathogens (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella). An alternative is respiratory fluoroquinolone monotherapy (levofloxacin 750 mg daily) if macrolide allergy or resistance concerns.' },
      { question: 'A CURB-65 score of 3 in a patient with CAP indicates:', options: ['A. Outpatient management with oral antibiotics', 'B. Consideration for hospitalisation, possible ICU', 'C. Observation only', 'D. Discharge with follow-up in 1 week'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'CURB-65 score 3-5 indicates severe pneumonia requiring hospitalisation, and ICU consideration for score ≥4. CURB-65 components: Confusion, Urea >7 mmol/L, Respiratory rate ≥30, Blood pressure <90/60, age ≥65. Score 0-1 = outpatient; 2 = consider admission; 3-5 = hospitalise. A CURB-65 score of 3 carries ~15% mortality at 30 days.' },
      { question: 'According to the WHO AWaRe classification, which antibiotic is classified as "Watch" and requires stewardship monitoring?', options: ['A. Amoxicillin', 'B. Ceftriaxone', 'C. Co-amoxiclav', 'D. Erythromycin'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'The WHO AWaRe classification categorises antibiotics into Access (first-line, narrow-spectrum), Watch (broader spectrum, higher resistance potential), and Reserve (last resort). Ceftriaxone is a Watch antibiotic. Amoxicillin and erythromycin are Access. stewardship programmes should monitor Watch antibiotic usage, with a target of Access:Watch ratio ≥2:1.' },
      { question: 'Why are blood cultures recommended before starting antibiotics in hospitalised CAP?', options: ['A. To guide duration of therapy', 'B. To identify bacteraemia and guide targeted therapy', 'C. To confirm viral vs bacterial cause', 'D. To assess antibiotic sensitivity of oral flora'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Blood cultures (2 sets from different sites) are positive in 10-20% of hospitalised CAP patients and identify the causative organism, allowing de-escalation from broad-spectrum empiric therapy to targeted narrow-spectrum treatment. This is a core antimicrobial stewardship principle and is particularly important in ICU patients and those with severe sepsis.' },
      { question: 'A 55-year-old smoker with CAP shows clinical deterioration at 72 hours despite IV ceftriaxone + azithromycin. What is the next best step?', options: ['A. Switch to oral antibiotics', 'B. Repeat chest X-ray, CT if needed, review sputum/blood cultures, consider resistant organisms or complications', 'C. Add metronidazole', 'D. Increase azithromycin dose'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Treatment failure at 72 hours requires systematic reassessment: repeat imaging (check for empyema, lung abscess, or parapneumonic effusion), review microbiology results, consider resistant organisms (MRSA, Pseudomonas), comorbidities (immunosuppression, aspiration), or alternative diagnoses (PE, malignancy). Broadening or changing antibiotics should be guided by clinical findings and culture results.' },
    ],
