    monitoring: 'Thick and thin blood smears at diagnosis, then every 12-24h in severe malaria until parasite clearance (expect >25% reduction at 24h, >50% at 48h). G6PD status before primaquine prescription. Random blood glucose every 6h in severe malaria. Hb at diagnosis and day 7. Serum creatinine and urea at baseline (severe malaria). Liver function tests. Temperature every 4-6h. Parasite count should clear by 48-72h with effective treatment — if not, consider drug resistance. Monitor for post-artesunate haemolysis: Hb drop and jaundice at days 7-14. ECG if QT prolongation suspected (lumefantrine).',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'First-line treatment for uncomplicated P. falciparum malaria in Kenya?', options: ['A. Chloroquine', 'B. Artemether/Lumefantrine (AL) 6-dose, 3-day course', 'C. IV Artesunate', 'D. Quinine + doxycycline'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Artemether-lumefantrine (AL) is the WHO-recommended and Kenya national first-line treatment for uncomplicated falciparum malaria. It is dosed as 6 doses over 3 days based on body weight. AL must be taken with fatty food to enhance lumefantrine absorption.' },
      { question: 'Which anti-malarial must be preceded by G6PD testing?', options: ['A. Artemether', 'B. Lumefantrine', 'C. Primaquine', 'D. Artesunate'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'Primaquine causes acute oxidative haemolysis in G6PD-deficient individuals, which can be fatal. WHO recommends G6PD testing before primaquine use for radical cure of P. vivax/P. ovale. In G6PD deficiency, use chloroquine for blood-stage and tafenoquine (if available) with caution.' },
      { question: 'What is the mechanism of action of artemisinin derivatives?', options: ['A. Inhibit heme polymerization', 'B. Endoperoxide bridge activation by haem iron generates free radicals damaging parasite proteins', 'C. Block dihydrofolate reductase', 'D. Inhibit DNA gyrase'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Artemisinin contains an endoperoxide bridge that is cleaved by Fe²âº from haemoglobin digestion in the parasite food vacuole. This generates cytotoxic free radicals that alkylate and damage parasite proteins and membranes, killing the parasite rapidly. Artemisinins act on all intraerythrocytic stages.' },
      { question: 'How should severe falciparum malaria be treated?', options: ['A. Oral artemether-lumefantrine', 'B. IV artesunate 2.4mg/kg at 0h, 12h, then daily', 'C. Oral quinine 600mg TID', 'D. IM artemether 3.2mg/kg single dose'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'IV artesunate is the WHO-recommended treatment for severe malaria, given at 2.4mg/kg IV at 0h, 12h, and 24h, then every 24h until the patient can take oral medication (usually 24-48h). It reduces mortality by 35% compared to quinine. Switch to oral ACT once patient can tolerate PO.' },
      { question: 'At what rate should parasite count fall in the first 24h of effective treatment?', options: ['A. 10% reduction', 'B. 25% reduction (or 15,000/Î¼L)', 'C. 50% reduction', 'D. 75% reduction'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'With effective antimalarial therapy, parasite count should reduce by ≥25% (or to ≤15,000/Î¼L) at 24h and ≥50% at 48h. Failure to achieve this indicates drug resistance or incorrect diagnosis and warrants change of regimen.' },
    ],
