    overview: 'Neonatal sepsis is a systemic infection occurring in the first 28 days of life, a leading cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality in Kenya. Early-onset sepsis (<72h) results from vertical transmission of Group B Streptococcus, E. coli, or Listeria monocytogenes, while late-onset sepsis (3-28d) is acquired from the community or hospital (S. aureus, Klebsiella, CoNS). Clinical features are non-specific: poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability, respiratory distress, and abdominal distension. Diagnosis relies on blood culture, CRP, and full blood count; lumbar puncture if meningitis is suspected. Empiric treatment is IV ampicillin 50mg/kg q6h + gentamicin 5mg/kg OD (extended interval), adjusted based on culture and sensitivity. Supportive care includes thermoregulation, IV fluids, and cardiorespiratory monitoring.',
