    overview: 'Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening systemic hypersensitivity reaction with rapid onset involving airway, breathing, and/or circulation compromise. Common triggers include foods (nuts, shellfish), insect stings, medications, and latex. Clinical features include urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm (wheeze, stridor), hypotension, syncope, and gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea). IM epinephrine 1:1000 (1 mg/mL) at 0.3–0.5 mg anterolateral thigh is the first-line, life-saving treatment and should be given immediately without delay. Up to 20% of patients experience biphasic reactions (recurrence 1–72 hours later), requiring 6–8 hours of observation. Second-line treatments include IV fluids, chlorpheniramine, and hydrocortisone, but these are never substitutes for epinephrine.',
    diagram: ANAPHYLAXIS_DIAGRAM,
    keyDrugs: [
      { drug: 'Epinephrine (Adrenaline) 1:1000 IM 0.3–0.5 mg anterolateral thigh', class: 'Sympathomimetic', sideEffects: ['Tachycardia', 'Hypertension', 'Tremor', 'Pallor', 'Myocardial ischaemia (overdose)', 'Ventricular arrhythmias'] },
      { drug: 'Chlorpheniramine IV 10 mg slow', class: 'Antihistamine (1st gen)', sideEffects: ['Sedation', 'Anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention)', 'Hypotension (rapid IV)', 'Dizziness', 'QT prolongation'] },
      { drug: 'Hydrocortisone IV 200 mg', class: 'Corticosteroid', sideEffects: ['Hyperglycaemia', 'Fluid retention', 'Immunosuppression', 'Psychosis (rare)', 'GI bleeding'] },
      { drug: 'Salbutamol nebuliser 2.5–5 mg', class: 'Beta-2 agonist', sideEffects: ['Tachycardia', 'Tremor', 'Hypokalaemia', 'Arrhythmias'] },
    ],
    monitoring: 'ABC assessment continuously; BP, HR, SpO₂, respiratory rate monitored every 5 minutes initially; ECG for cardiac ischaemia; response to IM epinephrine (repeat q5–15 min if no improvement); observe 6–8 hours for biphasic reaction (risk up to 20%); peak flow if bronchospasm present; urine output in severe cases; anaphylaxis trigger documentation and adrenaline auto-injector prescription at discharge.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'First-line, most important drug for anaphylaxis?', options: ['A. IV Chlorpheniramine', 'B. IV Hydrocortisone', 'C. IM Epinephrine 1:1000', 'D. Inhaled Salbutamol'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'IM epinephrine (0.3–0.5 mg, 1:1000, anterolateral thigh) is the only first-line treatment. Delayed administration is the most common cause of fatal anaphylaxis. Repeat every 5–15 minutes if no clinical response. H₁-antihistamines and corticosteroids are adjuncts only.' },
      { question: 'What percentage of anaphylaxis patients may experience a biphasic reaction?', options: ['A. 1–5%', 'B. 10–15%', 'C. 20% (up to)', 'D. 50%'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'Up to 20% of anaphylaxis patients experience biphasic reactions — recurrence of symptoms 1–72 hours (typically 1–8 hours) after the initial reaction. This is why 6–8 hours of observation is recommended even after symptom resolution.' },
      { question: 'Which route of epinephrine is preferred in anaphylaxis?', options: ['A. Subcutaneous', 'B. Intramuscular (anterolateral thigh)', 'C. Intravenous bolus', 'D. Sublingual'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'IM injection into the anterolateral thigh gives the most rapid and reliable absorption. SC absorption is slower and less predictable. IV bolus is reserved for cardiac arrest or refractory anaphylaxis under specialist supervision (risk of arrhythmias).' },
      { question: 'A patient with anaphylaxis is not responding to two doses of IM epinephrine. What is the next best step?', options: ['A. Give IV hydrocortisone', 'B. Start IV epinephrine infusion', 'C. Repeat IM epinephrine and start IV fluid resuscitation', 'D. Administer IV chlorpheniramine'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'Refractory anaphylaxis requires repeat IM epinephrine (q5–15 min), aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (1–2 L crystalloid rapidly), and if still unresponsive, IV epinephrine infusion (0.1–0.5 mcg/kg/min) with ICU admission and continuous monitoring.' },
    ],
