    id: 'dnote-cardiac-arrest',
    name: 'Cardiac Arrest',
    unitId: 'cp-em',
    specialty: 'Emergency & Critical Care',
    overview: 'Cardiac arrest is the sudden cessation of effective cardiac output, requiring immediate CPR and defibrillation for shockable rhythms. The algorithm distinguishes shockable rhythms (VF/pVT) from non-shockable rhythms (PEA/asystole). CPR quality is critical: compression rate 100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm, full recoil, 30:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio (until advanced airway placed). VF/pVT requires immediate biphasic defibrillation starting at 200 J, followed by 2 minutes of CPR, then reassess. IV epinephrine 1 mg every 3–5 minutes is given for both rhythm types. Amiodarone 300 mg IV bolus is given after the third shock for refractory VF/pVT. The 4 Hs and 4 Ts reversible causes must be systematically identified and treated: hypoxia, hypo/hyperkalaemia, hypothermia, hypovolaemia, tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins, and thrombosis (PE/ACS).',
    diagram: CARDIAC_ARREST_DIAGRAM,
    keyDrugs: [
      { drug: 'Epinephrine IV 1 mg every 3–5 minutes', class: 'Vasopressor', sideEffects: ['Tachyarrhythmia', 'Hypertension post-ROSC', 'Tissue ischaemia', 'Myocardial oxygen demand increase', 'Pulmonary oedema'] },
      { drug: 'Amiodarone IV 300 mg bolus (refractory VF/pVT)', class: 'Antiarrhythmic (Class III)', sideEffects: ['Hypotension', 'Bradycardia', 'Phlebitis', 'QT prolongation', 'Torsades de pointes (rare)', 'Pulmonary toxicity (long-term)'] },
      { drug: 'Lidocaine IV 1–1.5 mg/kg (alternative to amiodarone)', class: 'Antiarrhythmic (Class Ib)', sideEffects: ['CNS toxicity (seizures)', 'Bradycardia', 'Hypotension', 'Confusion', 'Numbness'] },
    ],
    monitoring: 'Continuous ECG rhythm monitoring; CPR quality metrics (compression depth, rate, recoil, hands-off time via feedback device); ETCO₂ (target >10 mmHg — sudden rise indicates ROSC); ROSC signs (palpable pulse, BP, ETCO₂ rise, SpO₂ return); temperature management post-ROSC (targeted temperature management 32–36 °C for 24 hours); arterial blood gas for pH and lactate; neurological assessment (GCS, pupil response) after ROSC;å† çŠ¶åŠ¨è„‰ perfusion pressure.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'Initial biphasic defibrillation energy for VF/pVT in adult cardiac arrest?', options: ['A. 100 J', 'B. 200 J', 'C. 360 J', 'D. 500 J'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Biphasic defibrillation starts at 200 J (monophasic: 360 J). If first shock fails, resume CPR for 2 minutes before re-analysing and delivering a second shock (escalating if needed). Early defibrillation within 3–5 minutes of VF arrest gives >50% survival.' },
      { question: 'Which of the following is NOT one of the 4 Hs and 4 Ts reversible causes?', options: ['A. Hypoxia', 'B. Tamponade', 'C. Thrombocytopenia', 'D. Tension pneumothorax'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'The 4 Hs: Hypoxia, Hypovolaemia, Hypo/Hyperkalaemia, Hypothermia. The 4 Ts: Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade (cardiac), Toxins, Thrombosis (PE/ACS). Thrombocytopenia is not a reversible cause of cardiac arrest.' },
      { question: 'In cardiac arrest with a non-shockable rhythm (PEA/asystole), when should epinephrine be given?', options: ['A. After 3 cycles of CPR', 'B. After the 2nd defibrillation', 'C. As soon as IV access is obtained (after first cycle of CPR)', 'D. Only after ROSC'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'For non-shockable rhythms (PEA/asystole), IV epinephrine 1 mg should be given as soon as IV/IO access is obtained. There is no role for defibrillation. CPR quality and reversible cause identification are paramount.' },
      { question: 'What compression-to-ventilation ratio is used in adult CPR before an advanced airway?', options: ['A. 15:2', 'B. 30:2', 'C. 30:1', 'D. 15:1'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'The standard ratio is 30 compressions to 2 breaths (30:2) for single or two-rescuer CPR before advanced airway placement. After endotracheal intubation, continuous compressions are performed with ventilations every 6 seconds.' },
      { question: 'Target ETCO₂ value indicating adequate CPR quality during cardiac arrest?', options: ['A. >5 mmHg', 'B. >10 mmHg', 'C. >20 mmHg', 'D. >30 mmHg'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'ETCO₂ >10 mmHg indicates adequate chest compressions and cerebral perfusion. A sudden rise in ETCO₂ (>40 mmHg) during CPR is a reliable indicator of ROSC. ETCO₂ <10 mmHg suggests inadequate CPR quality or poor prognosis.' },
    ],
