    overview: 'Psoriasis is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory skin disease characterised by well-demarcated erythematous plaques with silvery-white scale, caused by T-cell driven keratinocyte hyperproliferation (epidermal turnover 3–5 days vs normal 28 days). Plaque psoriasis (80–90%) is the most common form, typically affecting extensor surfaces, scalp, and intergluteal cleft. Severity is assessed by Body Surface Area (BSA), PASI score (Psoriasis Area and Severity Index), and impact on quality of life. Mild disease (<10% BSA): topical corticosteroids (betamethasone valerate 0.1% BD) ± vitamin D analogues (calcipotriol 50 mcg/g OD). Moderate-severe (>10% BSA or refractory): systemic therapy with methotrexate 7.5–25 mg weekly (with folic acid 5 mg weekly), ciclosporin 2.5–5 mg/kg/day, or biologics — anti-TNF (adalimumab, etanercept), anti-IL-17 (secukinumab, ixekizumab), anti-IL-23 (guselkumab, risankizumab). Biologics require TB screening (Quantiferon), hepatitis B/C serology, and vaccination updates before initiation.',
