    name: 'Paracetamol Overdose',
    unitId: 'cp-tox',
    specialty: 'Toxicology & Poison Management',
    diagram: PARACETAMOL_OD_DIAGRAM,
    overview: 'Paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose is the leading cause of acute liver failure in the UK, USA, and many other countries. Toxic dose: >150 mg/kg or >7.5 g in adults. Normally, paracetamol is metabolised by glucuronidation and sulphation, with a small fraction metabolised by CYP2E1 to the toxic metabolite NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine), which is detoxified by glutathione. In overdose, glucuronidation/sulphation pathways saturate, more NAPQI is produced, glutathione is depleted, and NAPQI causes hepatocellular necrosis (centrilobular). Diagnosis requires serum paracetamol level at ≥4 hours post-ingestion, plotted on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram. N-Acetylcysteine (NAC) is the antidote — most effective within 8 hours but should be started even beyond 24 hours. IV NAC protocol: 150 mg/kg in 200 mL 5% dextrose over 1 hour, then 50 mg/kg over 4 hours, then 100 mg/kg over 16 hours (total 300 mg/kg over 21 hours).',
    keyDrugs: [
      { drug: 'N-Acetylcysteine (NAC) IV 150 mg/kg over 1h then 50 mg/kg over 4h then 100 mg/kg over 16h', class: 'Antidote (glutathione precursor)', sideEffects: ['Nausea/vomiting (10–15%)', 'Anaphylactoid reaction (flushing, bronchospasm, hypotension — 5–10%)', 'Rash/urticaria', 'Hypotension (rapid infusion)', 'Non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (rare)', 'Headache'] },
      { drug: 'Methionine PO 2.5 g q4h for 4 doses (alternative if early and NAC unavailable)', class: 'Amino acid (glutathione precursor)', sideEffects: ['Nausea/vomiting', 'GI upset', 'CNS depression (rare)'] },
    ],
    monitoring: 'Serum paracetamol level at ≥4 hours post-ingestion, plotted on Rumack-Matthew nomogram (repeat at 12h if <150 mg/L at 4h but ingestion time uncertain); LFTs (ALT, AST) at baseline then every 12–24 hours; INR/PT at baseline and q12–24 hours (coagulopathy = severe hepatotoxicity); Cr and eGFR (renal impairment may occur with liver failure); blood glucose (hypoglycaemia in liver failure); blood gas (pH, lactate — acidosis indicates severe toxicity); ammonia (hepatic encephalopathy); clinical assessment: right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, hepatic encephalopathy, signs of chronic liver disease; King\'s College criteria for liver transplant assessment.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'When should NAC be started for paracetamol overdose?', options: ['A. Only when ALT is elevated', 'B. Within 8 hours of ingestion if level above treatment line on nomogram', 'C. After 24 hours only', 'D. Only if INR is prolonged'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'NAC is most effective within 8 hours of ingestion (>99% liver protection). Start immediately if paracetamol level is above the treatment line on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram. NAC is still beneficial beyond 24 hours (reduces mortality in late presenters with hepatotoxicity).' },
      { question: 'What is the toxic metabolite responsible for hepatotoxicity in paracetamol overdose?', options: ['A. Paracetamol glucuronide', 'B. NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine)', 'C. Paracetamol sulphate', 'D. Acetaminophen'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'NAPQI is the toxic metabolite produced by CYP2E1. Normally, glutathione conjugates NAPQI to non-toxic mercapturic acid. In overdose, glutathione is depleted (>70%), and NAPQI accumulates → centrilobular hepatic necrosis. NAC replenishes glutathione stores.' },
      { question: 'What is the total dose of IV NAC in the standard 21-hour protocol?', options: ['A. 150 mg/kg', 'B. 200 mg/kg', 'C. 300 mg/kg', 'D. 500 mg/kg'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'Total IV NAC dose is 300 mg/kg over 21 hours: 150 mg/kg in 200 mL 5% dextrose over 1 hour, 50 mg/kg in 500 mL over 4 hours, 100 mg/kg in 1000 mL over 16 hours. Anaphylactoid reactions are most common during the first hour infusion.' },
      { question: 'Which criteria determine need for liver transplant referral in paracetamol-induced liver failure?', options: ['A. ALT >1000 IU/L', 'B. King\'s College Criteria (arterial pH <7.3 or INR >6.5 + Cr >300 Î¼mol/L + Grade III/IV encephalopathy)', 'C. Bilirubin >200 Î¼mol/L alone', 'D. AST >5000 IU/L'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'King\'s College Criteria determine transplant listing: (1) Arterial pH <7.3 after resuscitation, OR (2) INR >6.5 (PT >100s) + Cr >300 Î¼mol/L + Grade III/IV hepatic encephalopathy. Single abnormal LFTs alone are insufficient for transplant assessment.' },
    ],
