    unitId: 'cp-tox',
    specialty: 'Toxicology & Poison Management',
    overview: 'Snake bite envenoming is a medical emergency, particularly in tropical and subtropical regions. In Kenya and East Africa, important species include saw-scaled viper (Echis), puff adder (Bitis arietans), black mamba (Dendroaspis polylepis), cobra (Naja), and boomslang (Dispholidus). Envenoming syndromes: cytotoxic (local swelling, necrosis — vipers), neurotoxic (ptosis, respiratory paralysis — elapids: mamba, cobra), haemotoxic/coagulopathic (venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy — vipers), and cardiotoxic. Diagnosis of envenoming: 20-minute whole blood clotting test (20WBCT) at bedside — unclotted blood at 20 minutes indicates venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy (VICC). Management: ABC resuscitation, immobilise affected limb (pressure immobilisation bandage for neurotoxic bites only), antivenom (polyvalent for African species) — give IV polyvalent antivenom for confirmed or suspected envenoming with systemic effects. Anaphylaxis to antivenom occurs in 10–50% (treat with epinephrine). Avoid tourniquets, incision, suction, ice, and electrical therapy. Wound care and tetanus prophylaxis. Observation for 24–48 hours (delayed neurotoxicity possible).',
    diagram: SNAKE_BITE_DIAGRAM,
    keyDrugs: [
      { drug: 'Polyvalent antivenom IV 1–2 vials (10 mL each) in 200 mL NS over 30 min', class: 'Antivenom (equine F(ab\')2)', sideEffects: ['Early anaphylactic reaction (10–50%: urticaria, bronchospasm, hypotension — treat with epinephrine)', 'Serum sickness (days 5–14: rash, fever, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy)', 'Hypotension (infusion-related)', 'Pyrexial reaction'] },
      { drug: 'Epinephrine 1:1000 IM 0.3–0.5 mg (for antivenom anaphylaxis)', class: 'Sympathomimetic', sideEffects: ['Tachycardia', 'Hypertension', 'Tremor', 'Arrhythmias'] },
      { drug: 'Tetanus toxoid IM (if not up to date)', class: 'Vaccine', sideEffects: ['Local injection site reaction', 'Fever', 'Arthralgia (rare)'] },
      { drug: 'Pethidine IV/IM 1–1.5 mg/kg (pain management)', class: 'Opioid analgesic', sideEffects: ['Respiratory depression', 'Nausea/vomiting', 'Seizures (normeperidine metabolite)', 'Sedation'] },
    ],
    monitoring: '20WBCT repeated q6h for 24–48 hours (unclotted at 20 min = positive = ongoing envenoming); bleeding signs (gums, wound oozing, haematuria, melaena); progression of local swelling (circumferential limb measurements q1–2h); neurotoxic signs (ptosis, diplopia, bulbar palsy, respiratory weakness — vital capacity monitoring); FBC, INR/aPTT, fibrinogen, Cr at baseline and q6–12h; urine output; vital signs continuous; wound assessment for necrosis; compartment pressure if tense swelling; anaphylaxis watch during antivenom infusion (first 30 min highest risk); cross-match if significant bleeding.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'First-line bedside test for venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy (VICC)?', options: ['A. INR/PT', 'B. 20-minute whole blood clotting test (20WBCT)', 'C. aPTT', 'D. D-dimer'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'WHO recommends 20WBCT as the bedside gold standard for VICC. Collect blood in a plain glass tube at room temperature — if unclotted at 20 minutes, it is positive (indicating venom-induced defibrinogenation and coagulopathy). INR may be unreliable due to venom interference with the assay. Repeat q6h to assess response to antivenom.' },
      { question: 'When is pressure immobilisation bandage (PIB) recommended for snake bite?', options: ['A. All snake bites', 'B. Neurotoxic snake bites (elapids) only', 'C. Cytotoxic viper bites only', 'D. Never recommended'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Pressure immobilisation bandage (firm bandage over the bite + splint) is recommended for neurotoxic elapid bites (mamba, cobra) to slow lymphatic transport of venom to systemic circulation. It is NOT recommended for cytotoxic viper bites (can worsen local necrosis). In East Africa, PIB is applied while arranging transfer to a facility with antivenom.' },
      { question: 'How is anaphylaxis to antivenom treated?', options: ['A. Stop antivenom and give IV hydrocortisone', 'B. Slow the infusion rate and continue', 'C. IM Epinephrine 1:1000 0.3–0.5 mg + stop antivenom temporarily', 'D. Give IV chlorpheniramine only'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'Anaphylaxis to antivenom occurs in 10–50% of patients. Treatment: STOP antivenom temporarily, give IM epinephrine 1:1000 (0.3–0.5 mg anterolateral thigh), IV fluids, and antihistamines. Once stabilised, antivenom can be restarted at a slower rate with premedication (hydrocortisone + chlorpheniramine). Life-threatening reactions require ICU care.' },
      { question: 'Which organism is responsible for venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy in Kenya?', options: ['A. Black mamba', 'B. Saw-scaled viper (Echis)', 'C. Boomslang', 'D. Puff adder'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Saw-scaled viper (Echis carinatus) is the most common cause of VICC in Kenya/East Africa. Its venom contains procoagulant enzymes that activate prothrombin and Factor X, consuming clotting factors and causing a DIC-like coagulopathy. Boomslang causes venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy as well (Type I anticoagulant venom).' },
    ],
